## Context

discover is where an agent first meets the AIDE convention in practice. Every other tool is called in response to something discover revealed. Specs form a cascading intent tree: a root sets project-wide intent and every child narrows its parent, so an agent working on a leaf module inherits constraints from every ancestor between root and leaf. Without discover surfacing that lineage in one call, the agent walks the filesystem upward, opening specs one at a time, to reconstruct what it inherits — replacing that manual walk is discover's whole point.

## Strategy

**Two modes, one switch — the presence of `path`.** No path means a shallow project-wide map; with a path means a deep subtree scan opening with the ancestor chain. The switch is `path` itself, not a hidden flag, because that is the same field the agent is already using to scope its attention.

**Shallow is cheap on purpose.** Reading file contents in shallow mode would turn a cheap, predictable map into an unpredictable expense and force agents to guess when it is "safe" to call. The cheapness is the feature that earns the tool a place at every session start.

**Deep mode returns the ancestor chain before the subtree.** Inherited context must be established before the agent interprets the subtree's own specs. The chain is always part of a path-scoped response — never a separate flag — because a subtree without its lineage is a leaf without a branch.

**Description and status make the chain self-contained.** Each ancestor renders with its description and any non-default status, so the agent understands the lineage without opening any spec. Pending (the default) is not rendered — it is the absence of review, not a finding, and showing it everywhere drowns out the meaningful signals.

**Let the tool's own behavior teach the pattern.** An agent reading discover's description learns "call with no path for the map, call with a path to drill in and see your ancestor chain" — and that pattern *is* cascading intent through progressive disclosure. Discover is the only tool whose behavior is part of what it teaches.

## Good examples

An agent starts a session in an unfamiliar project and calls `aide_discover` with no arguments. It receives a flat tree of every spec location with type tags and no content read — enough to see the project's spec architecture and decide where to drill in next.

The same agent then calls `aide_discover` with a path. The response opens with an ancestor chain — three or four lines naming each ancestor, its description, and any alignment status — followed by the subtree of the requested directory. The agent has the inherited context and the focused subtree in a single call.

## Bad examples

A shallow call that has silently inspected file contents and rendered summaries beside each spec. The map is no longer cheap; agents that called it speculatively just paid for context they did not mean to spend.

A path-scoped call that returns only the subtree, omitting the ancestor chain. The agent now has to walk upward and call discover again at every parent level to reconstruct what would have been one call.

An ancestor chain that shows paths without descriptions. The agent sees the lineage but must `aide_read` every ancestor to understand it — turning the one call discover was built to make sufficient back into N calls.

## References

- `.aide/intent.aide` — project-level cascading-intent model and single-source-of-truth invariant discover must surface.
- `src/.aide` — delivery-channel architecture and the teaching-through-tooling constraint that shapes discover's output shape.
